1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:03,590
Antalya, Turkey.

2
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:05,140
Sunday night.

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:06,419
The longest climate summit ever held

4
00:00:06,439 --> 00:00:07,760
is two days past its deadline.

5
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:10,218
Nearly two hundred countries are in the room.

6
00:00:11,028 --> 00:00:13,078
Then the president of the summit says:

7
00:00:13,258 --> 00:00:16,168
I understand that there is no agreement on this text.

8
00:00:16,358 --> 00:00:18,619
And the main deal of the summit is gone.

9
00:00:19,519 --> 00:00:21,322
Now, here's the strange part.

10
00:00:21,342 --> 00:00:22,859
Almost forty years ago,

11
00:00:22,879 --> 00:00:25,707
the same world faced a danger in the sky.

12
00:00:25,727 --> 00:00:26,675
And it agreed.

13
00:00:26,695 --> 00:00:27,473
In the end,

14
00:00:27,493 --> 00:00:30,513
every single country on Earth signed up.

15
00:00:31,323 --> 00:00:34,963
Spray cans, fridges, air conditioners, all changed.

16
00:00:35,273 --> 00:00:36,053
And today,

17
00:00:36,113 --> 00:00:39,703
a hole that once opened over Antarctica is slowly closing.

18
00:00:40,613 --> 00:00:41,913
By the end of this video,

19
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:44,013
you'll know why the ozone deal worked,

20
00:00:44,203 --> 00:00:46,003
why climate is so much harder,

21
00:00:46,173 --> 00:00:47,623
and what that one success says

22
00:00:47,643 --> 00:00:49,250
about what could work next.

23
00:00:49,870 --> 00:00:50,036
So,

24
00:00:50,056 --> 00:00:52,680
why could the world fix one problem in the sky,

25
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:54,081
but not the other?

26
00:01:00,841 --> 00:01:01,691
Chapter one.

27
00:01:01,711 --> 00:01:03,109
One voice is enough.

28
00:01:03,789 --> 00:01:05,839
At a United Nations climate summit,

29
00:01:05,859 --> 00:01:07,319
there's no final vote.

30
00:01:07,449 --> 00:01:08,233
Not one.

31
00:01:08,643 --> 00:01:11,913
The climate treaty has one hundred and ninety-eight members.

32
00:01:12,193 --> 00:01:14,373
One hundred and ninety-seven countries,

33
00:01:14,413 --> 00:01:15,935
plus the European Union.

34
00:01:16,335 --> 00:01:17,995
Back in nineteen ninety-five,

35
00:01:18,015 --> 00:01:20,095
at the very first summit in Berlin,

36
00:01:20,155 --> 00:01:22,005
they couldn't agree on how to vote.

37
00:01:22,255 --> 00:01:24,835
The rule about voting was left in brackets.

38
00:01:25,015 --> 00:01:26,774
It's still in brackets today.

39
00:01:27,174 --> 00:01:29,774
So every decision is made by consensus.

40
00:01:30,054 --> 00:01:31,774
In theory, one country,

41
00:01:31,794 --> 00:01:33,674
out of one hundred and ninety-eight,

42
00:01:33,694 --> 00:01:34,818
can stop a deal.

43
00:01:35,638 --> 00:01:37,558
That's what happened in Copenhagen,

44
00:01:37,578 --> 00:01:39,328
in two thousand and nine.

45
00:01:39,718 --> 00:01:41,658
A handful of countries objected,

46
00:01:41,768 --> 00:01:43,188
and the summit could only take

47
00:01:43,208 --> 00:01:44,670
note of its own accord.

48
00:01:45,070 --> 00:01:46,062
And in Antalya,

49
00:01:46,082 --> 00:01:47,242
it happened again,

50
00:01:47,262 --> 00:01:48,254
from two sides.

51
00:01:48,274 --> 00:01:49,491
The European Union,

52
00:01:49,511 --> 00:01:50,109
the U K,

53
00:01:50,129 --> 00:01:52,751
and small island nations refused a text

54
00:01:52,771 --> 00:01:55,168
that didn't even mention fossil fuels.

55
00:01:55,188 --> 00:01:56,798
Reports say Saudi Arabia,

56
00:01:56,818 --> 00:01:59,384
speaking for the Arab Group, and Russia,

57
00:01:59,404 --> 00:02:01,503
resisted phasing them out.

58
00:02:02,403 --> 00:02:04,153
The UN chief put it this way:

59
00:02:04,413 --> 00:02:07,293
there may be no consensus in the negotiating room,

60
00:02:07,433 --> 00:02:09,877
but there is no ambiguity in the science.

61
00:02:10,877 --> 00:02:12,487
So yes, the rules are hard.

62
00:02:12,507 --> 00:02:14,220
But the ozone deal was made

63
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:16,054
by the same kind of countries,

64
00:02:16,074 --> 00:02:17,990
with the same kind of diplomats.

65
00:02:18,010 --> 00:02:20,291
So what made that one different?

66
00:02:21,471 --> 00:02:22,331
Chapter two.

67
00:02:22,351 --> 00:02:23,675
The hole in the sky.

68
00:02:24,355 --> 00:02:25,745
In nineteen eighty-five,

69
00:02:25,885 --> 00:02:27,667
three scientists at a station in

70
00:02:27,687 --> 00:02:30,015
Antarctica looked at their own numbers,

71
00:02:30,095 --> 00:02:31,423
and didn't believe them.

72
00:02:31,913 --> 00:02:36,463
First, the shield.

73
00:02:36,553 --> 00:02:36,943
About fifteen to thirty-five kilometres above your head,

74
00:02:36,963 --> 00:02:38,583
there's a thin layer of ozone.

75
00:02:38,913 --> 00:02:42,561
It soaks up most of the sun's harshest ultraviolet light.

76
00:02:42,961 --> 00:02:45,571
In nineteen seventy-four, two chemists,

77
00:02:45,601 --> 00:02:47,801
Mario Molina and Sherwood Rowland,

78
00:02:47,971 --> 00:02:50,231
warned that a group of chemicals called CFCs

79
00:02:50,251 --> 00:02:52,525
could drift up there and destroy it.

80
00:02:52,935 --> 00:02:54,313
CFCs were everywhere.

81
00:02:54,333 --> 00:02:56,060
In spray cans, in fridges,

82
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:57,982
in air conditioners, in foam.

83
00:02:58,002 --> 00:02:59,322
They seemed perfect.

84
00:02:59,342 --> 00:03:00,720
Cheap, safe to touch,

85
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:02,526
and they never broke down.

86
00:03:02,546 --> 00:03:04,921
Which was exactly the problem.

87
00:03:05,331 --> 00:03:08,151
Up high, sunlight finally breaks them apart,

88
00:03:08,291 --> 00:03:11,801
and each chlorine atom can destroy ozone again and again,

89
00:03:11,971 --> 00:03:13,306
thousands of times.

90
00:03:14,326 --> 00:03:22,546
Then, in May nineteen eighty-five, Joe Farman,

91
00:03:23,006 --> 00:03:23,407
Brian Gardiner and Jonathan Shanklin of the

92
00:03:23,427 --> 00:03:24,106
British Antarctic Survey published their data.

93
00:03:24,146 --> 00:03:26,886
Every October,

94
00:03:27,186 --> 00:03:27,706
the ozone over their station at Halley was dropping.

95
00:03:27,726 --> 00:03:28,414
By about a third.

96
00:03:29,444 --> 00:03:31,284
A hole, over Antarctica.

97
00:03:31,394 --> 00:03:32,974
And here's what happened next.

98
00:03:33,194 --> 00:03:34,474
Within two and a half years,

99
00:03:34,494 --> 00:03:36,333
the world signed a deal to fix it.

100
00:03:37,503 --> 00:03:38,423
Chapter three.

101
00:03:38,443 --> 00:03:39,866
How the world said yes.

102
00:03:40,546 --> 00:03:43,216
It wasn't because people were nicer in the eighties.

103
00:03:43,446 --> 00:03:45,545
It was because saying yes was cheap.

104
00:03:45,965 --> 00:03:48,845
On the sixteenth of September, nineteen eighty-seven,

105
00:03:48,885 --> 00:03:51,075
the Montreal Protocol was agreed.

106
00:03:51,465 --> 00:03:53,255
A plan to cut CFCs,

107
00:03:53,275 --> 00:03:54,463
step by step.

108
00:03:54,923 --> 00:03:56,302
First reason it worked:

109
00:03:56,322 --> 00:03:58,840
only a few companies made these chemicals.

110
00:03:58,860 --> 00:03:59,617
In the U S,

111
00:03:59,637 --> 00:04:00,601
there were five.

112
00:04:00,621 --> 00:04:02,258
And in nineteen eighty-eight,

113
00:04:02,278 --> 00:04:03,657
the biggest one, DuPont,

114
00:04:03,677 --> 00:04:05,921
said it would stop making them.

115
00:04:06,631 --> 00:04:10,191
Second reason: replacements already existed,

116
00:04:10,411 --> 00:04:11,901
or were close.

117
00:04:12,031 --> 00:04:13,681
Your fridge still worked.

118
00:04:13,801 --> 00:04:14,441
Your deodorant still sprayed.

119
00:04:14,461 --> 00:04:15,630
Daily life barely changed.

120
00:04:16,130 --> 00:04:17,730
Third reason: money.

121
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:21,140
A fund was set up to help developing countries switch.

122
00:04:21,390 --> 00:04:22,280
So far,

123
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:25,460
it has received pledges of over five billion dollars,

124
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:28,218
and helped one hundred and forty-four countries.

125
00:04:29,028 --> 00:04:30,398
And the treaty could be tightened,

126
00:04:30,418 --> 00:04:32,198
whenever the science got scarier.

127
00:04:32,278 --> 00:04:33,188
So it was.

128
00:04:33,278 --> 00:04:34,507
Again and again.

129
00:04:34,907 --> 00:04:36,537
In two thousand and nine,

130
00:04:36,567 --> 00:04:38,017
the last country joined.

131
00:04:38,327 --> 00:04:39,737
Every country on Earth.

132
00:04:40,177 --> 00:04:41,937
Together with its parent treaty,

133
00:04:42,077 --> 00:04:44,492
it became the first global environmental

134
00:04:44,512 --> 00:04:47,309
agreement ever ratified by every country.

135
00:04:48,209 --> 00:04:51,869
Today, nearly ninety-nine percent of the banned chemicals are gone.

136
00:04:52,169 --> 00:04:54,679
But here's the twist nobody expected.

137
00:04:55,859 --> 00:04:56,849
Chapter four.

138
00:04:56,879 --> 00:04:58,789
The accidental climate treaty.

139
00:04:59,469 --> 00:05:01,502
The ozone deal may have done more for

140
00:05:01,522 --> 00:05:04,062
the climate than any climate deal so far.

141
00:05:04,522 --> 00:05:05,344
First, a myth.

142
00:05:05,364 --> 00:05:07,398
Many people think the ozone hole

143
00:05:07,418 --> 00:05:09,135
is what's heating the planet.

144
00:05:09,155 --> 00:05:09,662
It isn't.

145
00:05:09,682 --> 00:05:11,786
They're two different problems.

146
00:05:12,186 --> 00:05:14,286
But they share some of the same culprits.

147
00:05:14,546 --> 00:05:16,876
CFCs don't just eat ozone.

148
00:05:17,106 --> 00:05:19,556
They're also powerful greenhouse gases,

149
00:05:19,656 --> 00:05:22,795
thousands of times stronger than carbon dioxide.

150
00:05:23,215 --> 00:05:24,435
In twenty twenty-one,

151
00:05:24,495 --> 00:05:28,125
scientists modelled the world without the Montreal Protocol.

152
00:05:28,595 --> 00:05:30,035
By the end of this century,

153
00:05:30,145 --> 00:05:31,334
it would have been about two

154
00:05:31,354 --> 00:05:32,675
and a half degrees warmer,

155
00:05:32,775 --> 00:05:35,127
on top of the warming we already have.

156
00:05:36,117 --> 00:05:38,587
Part of that comes from the gases themselves.

157
00:05:38,867 --> 00:05:40,397
And part comes from plants,

158
00:05:40,417 --> 00:05:42,317
burned by ultraviolet light,

159
00:05:42,387 --> 00:05:44,347
soaking up far less carbon.

160
00:05:44,767 --> 00:05:46,985
Then, in twenty sixteen, in Kigali,

161
00:05:47,005 --> 00:05:48,476
the treaty grew again.

162
00:05:48,496 --> 00:05:50,025
It now also cuts HFCs,

163
00:05:50,045 --> 00:05:52,549
some of the gases that replaced CFCs.

164
00:05:52,569 --> 00:05:54,671
That could avoid up to half a

165
00:05:54,691 --> 00:05:57,746
degree of warming by twenty one hundred.

166
00:05:58,186 --> 00:06:00,016
And the ozone layer itself?

167
00:06:00,296 --> 00:06:01,136
It's healing.

168
00:06:01,436 --> 00:06:02,616
Over most of the world,

169
00:06:02,636 --> 00:06:05,856
it should be back to nineteen-eighty levels around twenty forty.

170
00:06:06,016 --> 00:06:08,862
Over Antarctica, around twenty sixty-six.

171
00:06:09,672 --> 00:06:11,452
So if the world can do this,

172
00:06:11,572 --> 00:06:14,383
why can't it do the same with carbon dioxide?

173
00:06:15,563 --> 00:06:16,503
Chapter five.

174
00:06:16,523 --> 00:06:18,066
Why carbon is different.

175
00:06:18,756 --> 00:06:19,906
To fix the ozone,

176
00:06:19,926 --> 00:06:21,776
we changed the gas in a fridge.

177
00:06:22,056 --> 00:06:23,176
To fix the climate,

178
00:06:23,236 --> 00:06:25,430
we'd have to change almost everything.

179
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:26,330
Coal,

180
00:06:26,350 --> 00:06:27,106
oil and gas still supply about

181
00:06:27,126 --> 00:06:28,160
eighty-six percent of the world's energy.

182
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:34,850
They power cars, planes, factories, steel, cement,

183
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:36,845
and the lights you're watching this by.

184
00:06:37,255 --> 00:06:38,535
In twenty twenty-five,

185
00:06:38,645 --> 00:06:41,029
burning fossil fuels released a record

186
00:06:41,049 --> 00:06:44,025
thirty-eight billion tonnes of carbon dioxide.

187
00:06:44,305 --> 00:06:45,725
Not a few factories.

188
00:06:45,955 --> 00:06:48,217
Billions of chimneys and engines.

189
00:06:49,017 --> 00:06:49,927
And there's a trap,

190
00:06:49,947 --> 00:06:52,327
described back in nineteen sixty-eight.

191
00:06:52,567 --> 00:06:54,697
It's called the tragedy of the commons.

192
00:06:54,987 --> 00:06:56,857
If everyone shares one field,

193
00:06:56,897 --> 00:06:59,597
each farmer gains by adding one more cow,

194
00:06:59,617 --> 00:07:01,507
and everyone shares the damage.

195
00:07:01,807 --> 00:07:03,487
The sky is that field.

196
00:07:04,507 --> 00:07:06,897
The world first tried strict targets.

197
00:07:07,127 --> 00:07:08,547
In nineteen ninety-seven,

198
00:07:08,567 --> 00:07:12,117
the Kyoto Protocol set binding cuts for rich countries.

199
00:07:12,407 --> 00:07:13,937
The United States signed it,

200
00:07:13,957 --> 00:07:15,387
but never ratified it.

201
00:07:15,627 --> 00:07:17,209
Canada later walked out.

202
00:07:17,629 --> 00:07:18,899
So in twenty fifteen,

203
00:07:18,919 --> 00:07:20,399
Paris tried the opposite.

204
00:07:20,729 --> 00:07:22,779
Every country picks its own plan.

205
00:07:22,949 --> 00:07:24,519
Almost everyone joined.

206
00:07:24,769 --> 00:07:26,819
Keep warming well below two degrees,

207
00:07:26,839 --> 00:07:28,815
and aim for one point five.

208
00:07:29,225 --> 00:07:30,725
But add up those plans,

209
00:07:30,865 --> 00:07:34,000
and the U N's latest report says today's policies lead

210
00:07:34,020 --> 00:07:37,627
to about two point eight degrees by twenty one hundred.

211
00:07:38,647 --> 00:07:39,947
So is it hopeless?

212
00:07:40,057 --> 00:07:40,897
Not quite.

213
00:07:40,987 --> 00:07:43,562
Because the ozone story has one more lesson.

214
00:07:44,752 --> 00:07:45,493
Chapter six.

215
00:07:45,513 --> 00:07:47,138
Slow, but not stuck.

216
00:07:47,818 --> 00:07:49,338
Consensus is slow.

217
00:07:49,468 --> 00:07:51,579
But history shows it isn't always stuck.

218
00:07:51,979 --> 00:07:54,259
In twenty twenty-three, in Dubai,

219
00:07:54,279 --> 00:07:55,559
for the first time ever,

220
00:07:55,709 --> 00:07:58,045
nearly two hundred countries agreed on words

221
00:07:58,065 --> 00:08:00,489
they had avoided for almost thirty years:

222
00:08:00,769 --> 00:08:03,293
transitioning away from fossil fuels.

223
00:08:03,693 --> 00:08:04,733
In the same year,

224
00:08:04,753 --> 00:08:06,973
the world agreed, by consensus,

225
00:08:07,073 --> 00:08:09,323
on a treaty to protect the high seas.

226
00:08:09,643 --> 00:08:12,574
It came into force in January twenty twenty-six.

227
00:08:12,984 --> 00:08:14,434
And a myth worth fixing:

228
00:08:14,644 --> 00:08:16,707
twenty twenty-four was the first full

229
00:08:16,727 --> 00:08:18,914
year above one point five degrees.

230
00:08:19,194 --> 00:08:21,864
That doesn't mean the Paris goal is already lost.

231
00:08:22,144 --> 00:08:24,304
It's measured over twenty-year averages,

232
00:08:24,394 --> 00:08:27,637
and the long-term warming is still around one point three.

233
00:08:28,547 --> 00:08:30,887
Remember why the ozone deal was easy:

234
00:08:31,017 --> 00:08:32,807
the alternatives were ready and cheap.

235
00:08:32,827 --> 00:08:33,087
The harder the change,

236
00:08:33,327 --> 00:08:34,447
the harder the yes.

237
00:08:34,467 --> 00:08:37,866
And the cheaper the change, the easier.

238
00:08:38,686 --> 00:08:41,736
The next summit is in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia,

239
00:08:41,756 --> 00:08:43,466
in November twenty twenty-seven.

240
00:08:43,686 --> 00:08:44,816
Same rules.

241
00:08:44,916 --> 00:08:47,377
Same hundred and ninety-odd voices.

242
00:08:47,797 --> 00:08:47,962
So,

243
00:08:47,982 --> 00:08:50,606
why could the world fix one problem in the sky,

244
00:08:50,626 --> 00:08:52,007
but not the other?

245
00:08:52,437 --> 00:08:54,166
Because the ozone deal asked a

246
00:08:54,186 --> 00:08:56,467
few factories to change one chemical,

247
00:08:56,637 --> 00:08:59,177
with replacements ready and money to help.

248
00:08:59,597 --> 00:09:01,453
The climate deal asks every country

249
00:09:01,473 --> 00:09:03,785
to change the energy under everything.

250
00:09:04,695 --> 00:09:05,715
Same rules.

251
00:09:05,745 --> 00:09:06,685
Same people.

252
00:09:06,705 --> 00:09:08,809
A very different price of saying yes.

253
00:09:09,829 --> 00:09:11,619
On Sunday night in Antalya,

254
00:09:11,639 --> 00:09:13,829
the summit ended without its main deal.

255
00:09:14,109 --> 00:09:15,199
And high above it,

256
00:09:15,219 --> 00:09:17,118
the hole the world agreed to fix

257
00:09:17,138 --> 00:09:19,739
in nineteen eighty-seven is quietly closing.

258
00:09:20,079 --> 00:09:22,159
Proof that the world can say yes.

259
00:09:22,309 --> 00:09:23,661
It has done it before.

260
00:09:24,851 --> 00:09:27,981
If this helped,

261
00:09:28,231 --> 00:09:29,641
like the video,

262
00:09:29,771 --> 00:09:31,531
and subscribe to Whyverse.

263
00:09:31,551 --> 00:09:31,871
And for shorter stories,

264
00:09:31,891 --> 00:09:31,999
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

